</code></pre> <p>At <code>factor: 1.0</code> one simulated second takes one real second. The run stops at simulation time 20 (<code>until: 20</code>), so it takes 20 real seconds. Each record is one line: <code>[EVT]</code> lines are events, and <code>[TEL]</code> lines are telemetry. The only telemetry here is <code>system.simulation.lag_seconds</code>, published once per simulation second, which says how far the run is behind the wall clock. These are the events of the first 6 seconds:</p> <pre><code>[EVT] {'sim_ts': 0.6283113312939436, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'sim_id': 'Shop', 'service': 'packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 0.6283113312939436, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'sim_id': 'Shop', 'service': 'packing', 'status': 'started'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 2.1283113312939435, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-1', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 0, 'value': {'event_type': 'order_packed'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 4.934338901219139, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-2', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 1, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'sim_id': 'Shop', 'service': 'packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 4.934338901219139, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-2', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 1, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'sim_id': 'Shop', 'service': 'packing', 'status': 'started'}}
[EVT] {'sim_ts': 5.0244486188135875, 'timestamp': '...', 'key': 'orders-3', 'task': 'pack_order', 'instance': 2, 'value': {'path_id': 'Shop.service.packing', 'sim_id': 'Shop', 'service': 'packing', 'status': 'queued'}}
</code></pre> <p>Read <code>sim_ts</code>, the simulation time of each record. It does not count up in small steps: it jumps from 0.628 to 2.128, then to 4.934 and 5.024. The simulation clock moves straight to the time of the next event, because nothing changes between two events. At <code>factor: 0</code> the same run takes a fraction of a second, with the same records.</p> <p><code>timestamp</code> is the date and time of the record: it counts from the moment the simulation is created, so it differs on every run, and this page shows it as <code>...</code>. Logical Start Time sets a fixed date instead.</p> <p><code>--sample 5</code> shows the first records without waiting and without changing the file. It runs the blueprint unpaced, as fast as it can, prints its first 5 events to stdout as JSON lines (one record per line), and stops:</p> <pre><code>desflow run first_shop.yaml --sample 5
